        <body>
            <div xml:id="d301">

                <pb facs="0319" n="315" sameAs="frus:frus1938v03/pg315"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.94/14048: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Japan</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Grew</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Tokyo</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1938-10-12T15:00:00+09:00">October 12, 1938—3
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received October 12—8:20 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>660. Our 657, October 12, 10 a.m.<anchor n="33" xml:id="d301-f1"/></p>
                <list>
                    <label>1.</label>
                    <item>My British colleague understands that all preparations for landing
                        Japanese forces on the coast of Kwangtung Province for a drive on Canton
                        have been made for some time past and that the Japanese were only awaiting
                        certain circumstances to put the plan into execution. He believes that the
                        circumstances which have brought matters to a head are: (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">a</hi>) information that Chiang Kai Shek when he
                        leaves Hankow may proceed to the south instead of to the west, and (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">b</hi>) a desire to distract public attention in
                        Japan from the delays and occasional defeats incidental to the drive on
                        Hankow, or both.</item>
                    <label>2.</label>
                    <item>Craigie yesterday told the Vice Minister that a drive on Canton could have
                        a seriously adverse effect on Anglo-Japanese relations from <pb facs="0320"
                            n="316" xml:id="pg316"/> the following points of view: (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">a</hi>) the danger to Japanese residents in Hong
                        Kong from an enflamed Chinese populace; (<hi rendition="#italic">b</hi>) the
                        serious problem of Chinese refugees flocking into the colony; (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">c</hi>) the attendant problem of food supplies; and
                            (<hi rendition="#italic">d</hi>) the risk of incidents involving British
                        shipping. The Vice Minister merely replied that such of these points as
                        concerned the Japanese authorities on the spot would be referred to them for
                        consideration.</item>
                </list>
                <p>Repeated to Hong Kong for Chungking.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Grew</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
